Date: Mon, 8 Feb 93 17:55:07 EST
From: Andy Wilson <ajw@Think.COM>
Subject: Political Prisoners

   Date: Mon, 8 Feb 93 16:23:07 EST
   From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU

   It's nice to get to the heart of things:

	...

   A political prisoner is someone who is jailed for advocating something
   that the current regime doesn't like.  That is, it's for disseminating
   politically incorrect opinions.  People who have committed other
   illegal acts aren't political prisoners, even though they may have
   attracted extra attention from the authorities because they *did*
   advocate ideas that the regime didn't like.

This is rather naive.  If a government wants to imprison people for
political reasons,  why not just frame them?

   Thus, of the above list, Leary was an ordinary criminal, while the
   rest were political prisoners.

G. Gordon Liddy admitted to having planted the marijuana after Leary was
released.  If someone is jailed ostensibly for a crime that they did not 
commit,  when they are voicing "incorrect" opinions,  I think you
can connect the dots.  Dr. Leary's autobiography contains a copy of a
letter from the Swiss government declining to extradite Leary because
they felt he was a political prisoner.  Part of what led them to this
conclusion was the fact that he was sentenced to an unprecedented
term for the crime,  30 years.  The prosecutor argued that he should
get this sentence because he was a "danger to society".

Note that at the time he was arrested he was running for Governor of
California against Ronald Reagan.  His campaign was,  interestingly
enough,  rather libertarian in nature.

There are all kinds of ways to imprison people for political purposes,
while having it look to everyone else that the person is a criminal.

Andy


