Dale Worley writes:
> The odd thing (from what I see) is that libertarianism is usually
> "sold" as promoting economic growth (which can be doubted, as can any
> economic theory), rather than as increasing personal freedom (which is
> true by definition).  The latter approach would probably make
> libertarianism's virtues more attractive to the general public -- I
> think most people would sign on to a doctrine that promised dramatic
> increases in personal freedom, in return for an at most small loss in
> cash income.  Emphasizing the positive economic benefits automatically
> leads into a swamp where nothing can really be proved.

Unfortunately, I can't agree that the general public would be any more
likely to accept libertarian ideas if they were sold as freedom-maximizing.
I've come to the unhappy conclusion that most people aren't interested
in extending real freedom to their neighbors; they don't trust their
neighbors with freedom and they're not so sure what they would do with
it themselves, either.

The fact that extropians are a desparately small minority is testament to
this view. If everybody loved freedom, we wouldn't need a list like this.
We live in an unhappy time dominated by the bloated middle-age of a republic
fast becoming empire (U.S.A.) and the greatest likelyhood is that it
will outlast all of us by far. (I know that one of the extropian principles
is radical life extension, but my own thoughts are that such techniques,
if discovered, are unlikely to be available soon enough for someone now
older than 25 years, as an upper bound.)

Obviously, I would love to be proven wrong; this argument is depressing. So
please -- if you've got a counter-argument to offer, I'd really like to
hear it.

Elias Israel
