Date: Mon, 8 Feb 1993 22:40:45 -0500
From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: Property as social system

Kryzs':

>> I claim that land can't be owned in absence of a social or legal system.
>> Nothing can be owned in absence of rules of ownership

Perry:

>No one would disagree -- however, note that in a PPL environment the rules
>of ownership are themselves decided on by a market process

So it falls on me, fnerd, a non-entity, to partially disagree.  Ownership 
can be as simple as holding on to something and fighting others off.  Or 
marking an area and establishing a pattern of defending it.  Don't
overlook these simple examples where, for one thing, the beneficiary pays 
for the enforcement.  I like to think of other forms of property as 
extensions of these simple kinds--someone has convinced others to stay 
away from something he or she wants to keep.

When the rules or social or legal systems stray too far from this basis,
then I start to wonder whether it's really "property" that's being
enforced.

Dale responding to Kryzs' same paragraph:

>Personally, I agree.  But it seems to me that libertarians have
>universally claimed that ownership is logically prior to social or
>legal systems.  Otherwise, how could such a system "steal" property
>via taxation, if it is the mechanism which definese ownership?

(I think minarchist and Lib Party libs are more likely to take the position
that property is logically prior.  Ironic if the more "moderate" and
"gradualist" libs have the more black-and-white moralistic theories.)

But in defense of that idea, you can say that what makes a good vs. a bad
social system or set of rules, is logically prior to any given system.

