Date:     Tue, 9 Feb 93 7:57:08 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: ECON: Certainty of Proof

Once upon a time, the people of the USA believed that personal freedom
included economic liberties and property rights.  Then they got conned
into thinking they'd get a lot out of giving up economic liberties and
property rights, while their personal freedoms would remain intact.
This big a fraud took a long time, but it was complete by 1937.

The truth is, Statism was passed off on the people via economic arguments,
and bad ones at that.  Now we have lots of people who want personal freedom
and the benefits they've been promised by economic con men.  They don't
realize that the means by which the con men propose to achieve their
goals are antithetical to freedom and economic benefits.

Frankly, I start off selling liberty on moral grounds all the time.  I'm
always pushed into economic arguments, though, because of the sophistries
that people have swallowed lock, stock, & barrel as pretext to justify
the curtailment of their birthrights.

Licensure, which Garrett Goebel touches on, is a perfect example.  Licensure
is the practice of requiring people to have a permit to do business in a
certain occupation, or else.  Its pretext is that it prevents fraud and
protects consumers from malpractice.  Many occupations from doctors to
hairdressers and plumbers have licensure applied to them.

It doesn't apply to those who don't take money for their services.  If I
give you medical advice gratis, I'm not subject to medical licensure.  If
I ask for a fee, I am.

It has little to do with the quality of service provided.  Studies have
found no statistically significant difference between the performance of
nurses who were forced to spend 4 years in nursing school versus ones who
were only forced to spend 3, for example.

What it does have to do with is monopoly profits for the licensed by
suppressing competition.  The AMA is a perfect example of this: it got
control of accredidation requirements for US medical schools in about
1910.  It soon shut down half the schools in the country.  Few of the
schools that admitted blacks, Jews, or women remained, only those that
fit the Johns Hopkins model.  I strongly doubt that the USA has as many
medical schools per capita now as it did in 1909.

Tightening accredidation and licensing requirements correlates best with
a decline in average income for medical practitioners.  Licensed
occupations have shown greater resilience to recessions than other
kinds of jobs.

Garrett, you're damn right I'd permit doctors to performs surgery without
a license, and you'd better believe it.  Ending licensure would help
reduce medical prices for all, leading to better health for the poor.

The inevitable "what about making sure they're any good" argument is to
be addressed by the argument that we should have competitive private
certification of professionals, which would yield a diverse continuum
of robust quality and affordability, not the stagnant monoculture of
the State.  "The Rule of Experts" by David Young covers the case against
licensure better than I have here.

As for the certainty of economic argument, it's only uncertain if you
accept the positivist methodology that so many economists have.  If you
apply praxaeologic method, you come to conclusions that have the
certainty of Euclidean proofs, arrived at in the same way: deduction
from axiomatic premises.  Mises' action axiom is the axiomatic premise
in economics: human beings behave purposefully.  Not all the rest
follows - there are some subsidiary empirical assumptions - but much
does.

